Ta có: $2 \cos^2 x - 1 = \cos \left(5x + \dfrac{\pi}{3} \right)$
$\Leftrightarrow \cos(2x) = \cos \left(5x + \dfrac{\pi}{3} \right)$
$\Leftrightarrow \left[
\begin{array}{l}
2x = 5x + \dfrac{\pi}{3} + k2\pi\\
2x = -5x - \dfrac{\pi}{3} + k2\pi
\end{array}
\right.$
$\Leftrightarrow \left[
\begin{array}{l}
-3x = \dfrac{\pi}{3} + k2\pi\\
7x = -\dfrac{\pi}{3} + k2\pi
\end{array}
\right.$
$\Leftrightarrow \left[
\begin{array}{l}
x = -\dfrac{\pi}{9} + \dfrac{k2\pi}{3}\\
x = -\dfrac{\pi}{21} + \dfrac{k2\pi}{7}
\end{array}
\right. \quad (k \in \mathbb{Z})$
